1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,080
So if it rains,

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:04,520
I'll probably just stay inside and watch a film.

3
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:08,720
Hello and welcome to Conditionals with Georgie - that's me!

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:10,400
Conditionals can be tricky.

5
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:14,160
We sometimes think of them  like they're mathematical equations:

6
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:18,200
use this structure plus this structure, in this situation,

7
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,360
but not this situation.

8
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:25,040
But sometimes, it's best to just relax, get your head out of the books,

9
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:27,680
and hear how they're used in real life.

10
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:31,320
So, what is a conditional?

11
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:40,040
Conditionals have two parts - the condition or a situation,

12
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:44,560
and the possible consequences
or results of that situation.

13
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:46,960
If this happens, this happens.

14
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:48,160
Look:

15
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:51,520
Usually, when I finish work, I play guitar.

16
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:56,320
So, if I don't do that, then I kind
of feel a bit stressed out, maybe.

17
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:59,400
Now, there are different types of conditionals

18
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,120
that express different things.

19
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:07,000
The type in the first example expresses things that are generally true or facts.

20
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,160
If George doesn't play the guitar, he
usually feels stressed out, in general.

21
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:14,400
But what else can you do with conditionals?

22
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:19,600
Well, you can use conditionals to
express a likely future situation and

23
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:21,840
the possible consequences of that.

24
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:29,360
If all the Tubes are cancelled, then I will cry, and get three buses home.

25
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:33,000
If all the tubes - that's underground
trains - are cancelled,

26
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,120
which is a likely situation in London, Andrew will cry,

27
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,400
but only if the tubes are cancelled.

28
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:41,600
It depends on that condition.

29
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:45,920
You can talk about situations that are unlikely to happen in the future -

30
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:49,560
hypothetical scenarios - and the consequences of that.

31
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:54,800
If I won a million pounds, I'd probably go travelling all round the world.

32
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:58,200
Now, it's unlikely Pippa will win a million pounds,

33
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:02,520
but she's kind of imagining and dreaming about what she could do

34
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:04,360
if she won a million pounds.

35
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:05,880
Why weren't you at the meeting?

36
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:07,640
Because you didn't tell me about it.

37
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:10,080
If you'd told me about it, I would have been there.

38
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:13,080
You can use conditionals for past situations too.

39
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:17,280
So these are things that have already
happened, so you can't change them,

40
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:21,600
but you're imagining alternative
situations and their results.

41
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,320
So I've given you a quick overview of the main conditionals.

42
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:28,920
And, now later in the series, we'll look at them in more detail,

43
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:31,920
not forgetting about the mixed conditionals too.

44
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:34,200
Don't forget to check out our website

45
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:38,600
and download the free worksheet to
help you practise. See you next time!

